{"causes": ["The ongoing expansionist ambitions of the Roman Republic necessitated strong military and political leadership to secure its borders and extend its influence.", "The protracted and existential conflict with the Samnites for control of central Italy created a constant demand for experienced generals like Longus.", "The Roman political system, particularly the ability to elect individuals multiple times to consulship and appoint dictators during crises, allowed figures like Longus to repeatedly hold supreme command."], "effects": ["Longus's military victories, particularly at Tarracina, significantly contributed to the weakening of Samnite power and the expansion of Roman control in central and southern Italy.", "His repeated consulships and dictatorship reinforced the precedent of experienced military commanders holding high office during periods of intense warfare in the Roman Republic.", "The consolidation of Roman influence in Campania and Latium under leaders like Longus laid crucial groundwork for Rome's eventual dominance over the entire Italian peninsula."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "roman-republic", "sourceName": "Roman Republic", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "gaius-sulpicius-longus", "targetName": "Gaius Sulpicius Longus", "context": "Gaius Sulpicius Longus was a prominent statesman and general within the political and military structure of the Roman Republic during the 4th century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "gaius-sulpicius-longus", "sourceName": "Gaius Sulpicius Longus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "second-samnite-war", "targetName": "Second Samnite War", "context": "Gaius Sulpicius Longus actively led Roman forces as consul and dictator during critical phases of the Second Samnite War (326-304 BCE)."}, {"sourceSlug": "gaius-sulpicius-longus", "sourceName": "Gaius Sulpicius Longus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "rome-italy", "targetName": "Rome, Italy", "context": "Gaius Sulpicius Longus held his political offices and celebrated his triumph in Rome, the capital city of the Roman Republic."}, {"sourceSlug": "samnite-wars", "sourceName": "Samnite Wars", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "gaius-sulpicius-longus", "targetName": "Gaius Sulpicius Longus", "context": "The protracted Samnite Wars significantly shaped Gaius Sulpicius Longus's career, providing the context for his military commands and political ascendance."}, {"sourceSlug": "gaius-sulpicius-longus", "sourceName": "Gaius Sulpicius Longus", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "roman-victory-at-tarracina-314-bce", "targetName": "Roman Victory at Tarracina (314 BCE)", "context": "As consul in 314 BCE, Gaius Sulpicius Longus commanded the Roman forces that achieved a decisive victory over the Samnites at Tarracina."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Political Center"}, {"name": "Samnium, Italy", "role": "Campaign Region"}, {"name": "Tarracina, Italy", "role": "Site of Victory"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Gaius Sulpicius Longus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "gaius-sulpicius-longus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q857105", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaius_Sulpicius_Longus"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Gaius Sulpicius Longus was a critical figure in the Roman Republic's expansion during the 4th century BCE, securing key victories against the Samnites that directly contributed to Roman consolidation of power in central Italy and set precedents for military leadership.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-20T07:01:37Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39380250228", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Gaius Sulpicius Longus was an accomplished general and statesman of the Roman Republic who served as Consul thrice and dictator once during his career, triumphing once over the Samnites and achieving great political success.", "newValue": "Gaius Sulpicius Longus emerged as a pivotal military and political figure during the Roman Republic's formative expansion in the mid-4th century BCE, a period defined by intense conflict with the Samnites for control of central Italy. His career, spanning the turbulent decades leading up to the Second Samnite War, solidified Roman dominion over strategic territories and established precedents for martial leadership. As a scion of the patrician Sulpicia gens, Longus navigated the complex political landscape of Rome, ascending to the highest offices and demonstrating both military acumen and political influence crucial to the Republic's ascendance.

Longus's distinguished service included three consulships (in 337, 323, and 314 BCE) and one dictatorship (in 312 BCE), each marked by significant engagements. His consulship in 314 BCE, alongside Marcus Poetelius Libo, saw him lead Roman forces to a decisive victory over the Samnites at the Battle of Tarracina, an engagement that broke Samnite resistance in Campania and secured critical coastal access for Rome. This triumph was further punctuated by the capture of Sora, a strategically vital town, and the subsequent imposition of Roman authority, for which he was awarded a formal triumph, a rare and prestigious honor validating his military prowess and contribution to Roman expansion.

The legacy of Gaius Sulpicius Longus is intrinsically linked to the consolidation of Roman power in Latium and Campania, paving the way for eventual Roman hegemony over the Italian peninsula. His repeated election to the consulship, and particularly his dictatorship, underscored the Republic's reliance on experienced military leaders during existential crises. His campaigns against the Samnites contributed directly to the strategic encirclement and weakening of Rome's most formidable rival at the time, establishing a pattern of aggressive expansion that would define Roman foreign policy for centuries."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-20T07:01:37Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39380250228", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "350 BCE, Ancient Rome"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "350 BCE, Ancient Rome"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing expansionist ambitions of the Roman Republic necessitated strong military and political leadership to secure its borders and extend its influence.", "The protracted and existential conflict with the Samnites for control of central Italy created a constant demand for experienced generals like Longus.", "The Roman political system, particularly the ability to elect individuals multiple times to consulship and appoint dictators during crises, allowed figures like Longus to repeatedly hold supreme command."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-20T07:01:37Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39380250228", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "350 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "350 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Longus's military victories, p